When a small group of living beings (a small population) separate from the main population from which they came (e.g., if they cross a mountain range or on, or if they migrate to another island so that it will be difficult to get back), the environment often turns out to be different than they were in before.
This new environment has other resources and competitors. The new residents have therefore needed other features or adaptability to be a strong competitor compared to what they used to be.
Original residents have not changed. They still need the same adjustment as before.
Over, over time, when the new residents begin to adapt to their new environment, they begin to look less and less like the rest of the population.
Finally, after thousands or even millions of years, the two foundries will be so different from each other that it will no longer be possible to call them the same species.
This is called species formation, but simply means that a new species is created. Species formation is an unavoidable consequence and a very important part of evolution.
